-1.19}, "rating_margin": -8.0, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.17, "baseline_margin": -3.96, "analyst_adjustment": 0.96, "key_factors": ["R+7 district fundamentals in an open seat after Amodei's retirement", "Flippo's Bronze Star misrepresentation scandal drawing national coverage", "Close polling (average R+1.4) but thin, partly Democratic-sponsored samples", "Flippo's large fundraising edge over Benitez-Thompson"], "rationale": "The district's R+7 lean and Flippo's money advantage point to a Republican hold, but the Bronze Star scandal, a polling average near even, and a few Lean R ratings justify moving a few points toward the Democrat from the Likely R consensus. Markets still price the Republican around three-quarters, capping how far the forecast shifts.", "watch": "Whether post-scandal polling in October shows the story durably eroding Flippo's support or fading as a news cycle.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mark Amodei (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Northern Nevada's open 2nd District seat has been Republican-held for years, and the district's partisan lean sits about seven points to the right. With Mark Amodei retiring, Republican David Flippo enters as the favorite, backed by Trump's endorsement and a large fundraising advantage over Democrat Teresa Benitez-Thompson, who has raised modestly and drawn little outside help.\n\nThe race has nonetheless become unusually competitive. Flippo faces sustained scrutiny over claims about his Bronze Star, reported by major outlets, and limited polling shows a near-even contest, though the samples are small and partly Democratic-sponsored. Most forecasters still call the seat Likely or Lean Republican, and prediction markets give the Democrat roughly a one-in-four chance. The expected outcome is a Republican win by a modest margin, with real but limited downside risk.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.213, "margin": -4.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2977, "margin"